ective but luckily it's very  well studied with in the commuter vision  community and there are some pretty good  solutions for it so this can be broken  down into two sub steps basically first  we want to find descriptive features of  each image that can be compared between  the two and then we want to match  features that are similar between the  two images so opencv provides a lot of  different feature detection detectors  some of these are edge detection and  corner detection but these aren't really  what we want because they're not really  robust or descriptive so we can't match  corners that are found because it's just  finding corners so we want features that  are descriptive so the answer to that  problem would be using sift features  which stands for scale invariant feature  transform and these are robust to  obviously its scale invariant so they're  robust to scale as well as perspective  and they provide a rich description of  what is contained inside the feature  that is found so each sift feature gives  you a point and like a size that it  corresponds to as well as a 128  dimensional feature vector that  basically describes what is in that  feature that it's found and you can use  that to compare across features and find  similar  cheers so these are the sift features  calculated for each image on the Left  video frame and on the right are  matching image as you can see some of  the features correspond to a larger size  and some of them of course want to a  smaller size but the feature descriptors  will remain the same no matter the size  of them so like for example on the video  on the left if someone were to walk back  ten steps those features would still  have the same feature descriptors but  the sizes of the features would change  so once we have features for both images  we can match them and we do this with an  algorithm called flam which stands for  fast linear approximate nearest  neighbors so regular nearest neighbors  the basic process for that would be for  each feature in the match image you  would want to find the closest feature  in the video frame through Euclidean  distance and Euclidean distance is  basically just like if you have like two  points let's say you have two points in  a two-dimensional surface and you draw a  line between them the distance of that  line Euclidean distance is just  generalized to n dimensions so in this  case it would be 128 dimensions of the  feature space so we're finding the  closest straight line between two  features so that's regular nearest  neighbors and we decided to use the  approximate nearest neighbors algorithm  because it's a lot faster and we found  that we found that we don't need to find  exact matches most of the time and at  the approximate nearest neighbors as you  can see here does a pretty good job of  matching the similar features so once we  have those matches we need to find the  relationship  between the frames so the video frame  and the static image so how do we define  this relationship we can define it as an  affine transformation which is just a  transformation that preserves points  straight lines and planes but it can  account for changes in perspective so no  curvature can be added but it can  account for changes in perspective so if  you look at this image to the right each  leaf in that image can be obtained by an  affine transformation on another leaf so  reflection rotation scaling shear  mapping those can all be accounted for  by an affine transformation so how do we  parameterize an affine transformation  that would that would be with something  called an affine matrix which is just a  3 by 3 matrix that you can apply to an  image and get the affine transformation  that corresponds so the FI matrix is  what we need to find the FI matrix that  corresponds to going from the video  frame to the static image so how do we  find that out fine matrix there's a  function in opencv called fine kamagra  fee and basically what this functio